# What goes up to the server, as rsync filter rules: the one list behind every
# way the tree reaches /opt/lava.
#
#   deploy/deploy.sh         rsync --filter="merge deploy/rsync.filter" ...
#   deploy/ci/pack.sh        the same rsync into a staging copy, then a tarball
#   deploy/remote/lava-gate.sh  the same rules (its installed copy) when it
#                            swaps the tarball into /opt/lava
#
# On the receiving side an excluded path is also a protected one: rsync
# --delete leaves alone whatever these rules exclude, which is how .env, res/
# and anything else that lives only on the server survive every deploy.
#
# First match wins, so order matters: the tools/ include has to come before its
# exclude, or rsync never sees the scripts. tools/ holds ~80 MB of downloaded
# players; only its Python scripts are ours.
#
# recon/ is the decompiled client: our reading material, no business on a
# public host. .github/ is the CI that sends this tree, and is no business of
# the tree once it is there. The image itself takes only server/ and data/
# (.dockerignore).
#
# The two wiki excludes name directories, not a data/wiki* glob: the glob also
# takes wiki_links.json and wiki_items.json, which are run-time tables the
# server refuses to start without. Same two lines as .dockerignore, and they
# should stay the same two.
#
# .git without the slash: in a `git worktree` checkout it is a file, not a
# directory, and a directory-only rule would let it through.
- .git
- .github/
- .claude/
- res/
- dist/
- logs/
- recon/
+ tools/
+ tools/*.py
- tools/*
- data/wiki/
- data/wiki_world/
- data/lava.db*
- __pycache__/
- *.pyc
- .env
